      "SummaryText": ", and the asset is owned by Ascend. Of the 11 total units at the facility, seven handle chemicals covered under the RMP.  \n\n3.0 General Accidental Release Prevention Program and Chemical-Specific Prevention Steps\nThe Ascend Chocolate Bayou plant is an OSHA Voluntary Protection Plan (VPP) Star Site and ISO 14001 certified. Ascend endorses and supports the American Chemistry Council's Responsible Care program --an international initiative that has helped chemical makers achieve one of the best safety performances of any industry in North America.  \nWe take a systematic, proactive approach to preventing accidental releases of hazardous chemicals.  Our management systems address all elements of the OSHA Process Safety Management (PSM) and EPA RMPA regulations.  Chemical specific monitoring devices, response protocols, material of construction, process interlocks, and operator training combine to prevent hazardous releases and mitigate the risk of any potential release. APM used RMP*Comp to model potential consequences as required in the RMP rule.  The specifics of the Worst and Alternative cases are provided in the RMP update.  \n\n4.0  Five-year Accident History\nWe keep records for all significant accidental chemical releases that occur at our facility.   In the past five years, one incident involving materials covered under EPA's RMP rule has occurred to meet the reporting requirements set forth in the program, and is included in the RMP update. \n\n5.0 Emergency Response Program\nWe maintain an integrated contingency plan (ICP) that consolidates all of the various federal, State of Texas, and local regulatory requirements for emergency response planning.  The ICP, which is referenced in the RMP submittal, includes provisions such as coordination with local emergency responders, planning, trainings, drills, and alert systems.\n\n6.0 Ongoing Changes for Continuous Improvement to Safety\nThe following is a list of ongoing improvements that we 